what is the most iconic moment in all of  one piece  now you yourself might or might not have  a very specific answer to this question  however whenever people ask me hey ohara  what's your favorite one piece moment of  all time  there are always a bunch of scenes and  events that immediately come to mind  but ultimately i can never decide which  one i really like best  there's just so much greatness to choose  from here  from very emotional stuff over brilliant  comedy  all the way to some of the most badass  moments in anime  and so i thought to myself if it's so  hard for you to pick your favorite  moments  why not revisit some of them find out  why they are great  and then pick a favorite at first i was  thinking about making this video a list  video  and simply presenting my top picks but  then i thought to myself  that a video the top 10 most iconic  one-piece moments  really doesn't fit in well with what  we're trying to do here on the channel  it also sounded like there were probably  already at least a thousand or so of  these out there  and so i quickly scratched that idea and  went back to the drawing board  and by drawing board i mean of course i  got a cup of coffee  and watched some anime because i'm a  goddamn genius when it comes to  procrastinating  but then i really sat down and this time  i fell asleep i honestly was a bit  discouraged whether i would be able to  pull this off somehow or not  and was ready to simply ditch the idea  and focus simply on my analysis videos  after all that's what i'm actually good  at and then it  hit me why not do exactly that  why not analyze these scenes and see  what makes them tick  why they become so popular and  ultimately pick the most iconic moment  in one piece  apart from a couple of fight analyses i  don't think i've ever come across a  proper scene analysis for an anime  and so i welcome you to my new format  the iconic scene analysis series  i think we might have to work on that  name though  i'm super excited to try something new  and i hope that this format is something  you will be enjoying as well  so let's give it a go to break down  every scene we will be looking at things  like  theme composition framing body language  dialogue symbolism music and voice  acting in the anime  the role the scene plays in the  overarching storyline  and much much more and so for the very  first video in the series  i thought it might be appropriate to  start with a banger right out of the  gate  with undoubtedly one of my favorite  moments in the story  and one of the best if not maybe the  best in zorro's arc  the nothing happened seen on thriller  bark  the scene takes place shortly after the  straw hat's united effort to defeat  moria and his giganteous zombie oars  and stretches from chapter 483 to  chapter 486  and in the anime it's episode 377 i  think  in case you want to follow along luffy  is knocked out from his intense final  battle  and the rest of the crew as well as  loler and to men  are resting after they finally got their  shadows back  however right when they have defeated  one of the shichibukai  kuma suddenly appears on the island he's  been tasked to inform moria of  blackbeard replacing crocodile  of ace having been captured and of the  potential conflict with the whitebeard  pirates  however upon reporting that moria has  been defeated by luffy kumar receives  new orders  to capture the crew and kill all  remaining witnesses  so no word of moria's defeat can leak  into the world  to ruin the reputation of the warlords  even more  kuma then complements the unconscious  luffy on the crew he has gathered  to which all of the straw hats react in  a hilariously flattered way  despite the serious situation they're in  and despite the damage all of them took  zoro immediately prepares to fight kuma  to protect his crew  and for the very first time in this  scene mentions his willingness to die  fighting if necessary  kuma however easily repels each of soros  attacks  and introduces his unique devil fruit  powers to the crew that allow him to  repel  anything as kuma catches zoro by  surprise  sanji jumps in for the very first time  as well to help zorro  however kuma takes no damage from either  of the two  and after proving his superiority he  offers everyone present a surprising  deal that goes against his orders  he will let everyone present on the  island live in exchange for luffy's life  but without so much as a second thought  all of the straw hats  as well as lolar and to mem refuse and  stand between kuma and luffy  as a result kuma takes out everyone  gathered there  with an enormous shockwave and  approaches luffy on his own  however before he reaches him zorro who  seems to be the last one standing  stands in his way once again this time  he actually manages to inflict some  damage to the warlord  only to find out that he's indeed a  cyborg developed by dr vegapunk  and kumar reveals the secret government  project of the pacifista  having witnessed kuma's true strength  zoro realizes that there is  no way he can actually win this fight in  his condition and he bows down to offer  kuma his life  instead of luffy's bargaining his  ambition to become the greatest  swordsman  before kuma can react however sanji who  seems to be on the brink of losing  consciousness  steps in prepared to in turn now give  his life  to save both zoros and luffy's dream  zorro has made up his mind though  and acknowledging sanzi's gesture he  knocks him out with his sword  kuma then proposes another trial for  zorro  using the powers of his devil fruit he  expels all of luffy's pain and fatigue  and gives a portion of it to zorro  telling him that taking in all of it  would kill him zorro however takes in  all of luffy's pain without so much as a  second thought  kuma then acknowledges luffy's crew and  leaves the island  and after regaining consciousness sanji  hurries to zara's side  finding him standing straight in a  puddle of blood  upon asking what in the world has  happened here  zorro simply states that nothing  happened  one of the most iconic lines in the  series  a bit later luffy is shown miraculously  back at full strength  while chopper states that the now  unconscious and treated zorro  only closely escaped death finally sanji  stops two of lola's men  sharing what has happened to not  diminish soros sacrifice  so next to the obviously fantastic plot  in here  what makes the scene so damn good how  did it become one of the most memorable  moments for zorro  well the first thing we should look at  is how this scene fits in with the  overall story  and what purpose it serves this epilogue  scene with kuma on thriller bark  to me seems to have four mage  implications for the plot of one piece  first of all it's of course meant to  introduce kuma as a character into the  story  while at the beginning of the scene oda  implies him to be a cold and unforgiving  government loyalists  hammered home by his epithet the tyrant  over the course of events  it becomes clear that kuma is in fact  sympathetic towards luffy and the crew  despite his orders and grim threats to  take luffy's head  he ends up letting everyone and thriller  bark walk away freely  his affiliation with the revolutionaries  is also  already hinted at here when he's  name-dropping dragon  and so in just a few chapters we get an  enormous amount of character development  for kuma that is still relevant until  now  when we're thinking about his role  during the reverie arc  secondly the entire scene contains  enormous foreshadowing for the next four  arcs  kuma is the first one to mention ace's  capture by the marines  and the upcoming conflict with  whitebeard foreshadowing both the events  at imple down  and marine fort at the same time the  introduction of his powers and his  combat superiority towards the crew  heavily foreshadows the events at  sabaodi and finally the time skip by  indicating that the crew  is not strong enough to face even all of  the shichibukai  let alone the marine admirals or the  yonko  thirdly by testing their resolve the  entire  sequence serves as the ultimate proof of  the straw hat's loyalty towards luffy  before heading into the crew's dark  trilogy arc of defeat  these three things aren't as obvious  when reading or watching the scene  itself  but do a wonderful job as an underlying  message and vibe here  that really pays off in the story going  forward  however the last and undoubtedly most  impactful purpose of this moment  is the strong characterization it gives  to sanji and especially to zorro  establishing both of them as luffy's  left and right hand  and lifting zorro up to be to luffy what  really was to roger  more than a crew member more than a  friend and  even more than a first mate but a  brother standing side by side with luffy  and being a pillar for his captain to  lean and rely on  it is some of the best characterization  in the entire story  and even more than before zorro's  sacrifice  made him one of the most respected and  most beloved characters of the series  so now let's dive a little bit more into  the details here  and i'll be referencing both the anime  and the manga here  both of which i think have done a  fantastic job with this scene  and both of which have their innate bits  that they excel at here  what i think really stands out in the  scene in particular  even for animated manga is the  character's body language  and the fantastic dialogue here kuma's  appearance  switches between laid back and natural  such as sitting on the wall with his  legs crossed reading his book and  talking to the tendent mushi  and at other times moving and behaving  mechanically and stiff  the same goes for his lines that  alternate between robotic  very simple sentences and words on the  one hand  and quite eloquent and intelligent lines  on the other that hint at a smart and  compassionate person  oda wonderfully uses every aspect of  kuma to hint at the dichotomy of his  character  as an intelligent and friendly member of  the revolutionaries  and the brainless and unforgiving  machine following the world government's  orders  by switching between human and machine  the straw hats meanwhile  express nothing but unity loyalty and  determination through their body  language and their dialogue in the scene  all of them see the danger they're in  and yet all of them make it clear  through their words and general demena  that they will stand by luffy no matter  what  especially zorro and sanji leave  absolutely no doubt  that they see each other as luffy's  representation to protect the crew  and uphold his determined and unwavering  will in his death  in more than just one scene zoro's face  and the heavy breathing included into  his lines  hammer home the feeling that he's  already way beyond his last reserves of  energy  and is mostly running on willpower and  determination alone  nonetheless the look in his face when  stepping into the bubble of pain  suggests nothing else than zorro being  fully prepared  to die here without any regrets  it's a very powerful visual depiction of  emotion and motivation  that the manga does an especially  amazing job with  and i can't even begin to describe how  powerful zoro's pose at the end is  covered in blood and at the brink of  death the anime meanwhile does  a wonderful job in terms of sound design  and music in the scene  the build up to the climax is  accompanied by uneasy orchestral music  giving the scene a threatening cinematic  and epic feel  and once sanji regains consciousness and  hurries to find zorro  there is no music at all implying  something terrible has happened  only to hear the epic theme of the very  strongest starting to play  and seeing zoro's defined face absolute  goosebump material here  there is also a nice amount of symbolism  in the scene the most  striking however is without any doubt  kuma removing  all of luffy's pain and fatigue and  shaping it into an enormous red bubble  making it visible to us and to zorro  what luffy endures every arc for his  crew's sake  at the same time zorro literally  embracing luffy's pain and taking it in  is a wonderful symbol for the  relationship he has with his captain  beautifully executed here the last thing  i want to look at  is the framing in the scene all  throughout the sequence  kuma constantly towers over everyone  else  representing his superiority and the  helplessness of the crew  he is literally an obstacle too big to  overcome  and this consistency in his framing is  only broken  one soro offers his life to kuma in  luffy's stats  which then shows both of them at the  same level  through his words zorro has literally  earned to look eye to eye with kuma  and has passed his test for the crew  some other great compositional scenes  include sanji standing between zoro and  kuma and zorro  bowing down before kuma to back for  luffy's life all of which create an even  more emotional and impactful atmosphere  than this moment already carries  and ultimately zorro bleeding with his  arms crossed  is shown from below his waist after his  sacrifice  oda has lifted him to higher moral  grounds and giving him the perspective  that kuma had throughout the scene so  while each of the elements here  plot dialogue composition etc is  beautifully crafted by oda  it's the sum of these elements that  together create a moment that surpasses  its individual parts  it creates one of the most emotional  inspirational and suspenseful moments of  the series  and is a prime example of what makes one  piece so great  if you made it to this point please let  me know your thoughts on the new format  and if you would like me to keep it  going thanks guys  peace
 

